tative result against the source data.\n---\n\n# Review a numerical result\n\nUse after the analysis has produced a number someone will rely on.\n\n1. Read the request and the saved result. Write down what the main number measures, its units, the population it describes and the form the reader requested.\n2. Start a fresh calculation from the source. Use a different route from the draft where possible. For an aggregate, keep the observations or group totals that contributed. For a comparison, reconstruct each side before calculating the difference.\n3. Predict a sign, range or limiting case and check it. If the two results disagree, trace the first discrepancy to a record or a method choice. Change the draft only when the evidence settles it.\n4. Open the final deliverable. Check that its values, units and conclusion agree with the calculation and that the requested values can be read directly.\n\nA matching total can hide offsetting errors. Inspect a contributing observation when the calculation has several stages. Keep working notes outside a machine-readable answer.\n\n## Exercise\n\nReview the [assay analysis](../../benchmarks/tasks/assay/). Recompute a reported value directly from the measurements and compare the records contributing to both calculations.\n","program":"---\nname: review-an-analysis-program\ndescription: Test a drafted scientific program on fresh inputs through the caller's actual entry point.\n---\n\n# Review an analysis program\n\nUse when another scientist will run the delivered code on their own data.\n\n1. Find the promised file and entry point. Load the saved implementation as a caller would. A code block in a conversation cannot satisfy a file-based interface.\n2. Construct a small valid input with a result you can calculate independently. Predict the result before running the program. Follow what it reads from its arguments, files or configuration.\n3. Change one meaningful input. Predict what should change and what should stay the same. Run both cases against the saved code and compare actual outputs with those predictions.\n4. If a case fails, trace the first disagreement, make the smallest justified repair and rerun both cases. Deliver the implementation at the requested location.\n\nChoose input changes the scientific method permits. Reordering observations is meaningful for some analyses. Record the test in working notes when the requested output is code only.\n\n## Exercise\n\nUse the [seeded simulation](../../examples/pin-the-environment/). Run its existing entry point with a fixed seed, then change a caller-supplied parameter and check the expected behavior.\n","protocol":"---\nname: review-a-protocol-decision\ndescription: Verify that a drafted decision follows the controlling protocol and the supplied evidence.\n---\n\n# Review a protocol decision\n\nUse after an analysis has selected observations, applied a rule or declared that a result cannot be determined.\n\n1. Find the passage that governs the decision. Identify the unit it applies to, the evidence it requires and any condition that changes its scope.\n2. Work forward from the original records. For one accepted record and one nearby rejected record, write down the evidence and the clause that decides each. If all records receive one decision, check the closest boundary case and explain why it belongs there.\n3. Apply the same reasoning to the full population. Reconcile the selected identifiers or counts with the saved result, then follow any change through to the conclusion. For an unresolved decision, identify the missing fact and check whether another supplied source provides it.\n4. Read the delivered answer against the request. Preserve decisions supported by the evidence. If two readings still matter, state which source or clarification would settle them.\n\nKeep the evidence trail in working notes when the requested deliverable has a fixed format.\n\n## Exercise\n\nUse the [research contract](../../benchmarks/tasks/contract/). Trace one proposed interpretation to a specific source and identify which conclusion the supplied data support.\n"}},"jobs":[{"case":"toothgrowth-response-fractions","model":"terra","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"warpbreaks-loom-tail","model":"terra","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"warpbreaks-loom-risk","model":"terra","rep":1,"reviews":["ordinary","skill"]},{"case":"fin-goog","model":"terra","rep":1,"reviews":["ordinary","skill"]},{"case":"esoph-ordinal","model":"sonnet","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"fin-nvda","model":"luna","rep":1,"reviews":["ordinary","skill"]},{"case":"theoph-exposure","model":"sonnet","rep":1,"reviews":["ordinary","skill"]},{"case":"fin-msft","model":"terra","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"attenu-station-releases","model":"luna","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"warpbreaks-fleet-change","model":"luna","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"orchardsprays-latin-square","model":"terra","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"warpbreaks-fleet-change","model":"terra","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"morley-repeatability","model":"luna","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"attenu-attenuation","model":"sonnet","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"fin-amzn","model":"sonnet","rep":1,"reviews":["ordinary","skill"]},{"case":"orchardsprays-spill-panel","model":"sonnet","rep":1,"reviews":["ordinary","skill"]},{"case":"esoph-adjusted-odds","model":"sonnet","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"morley-drift-alarm","model":"luna","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"orchardsprays-spill-panel","model":"luna","rep":1,"reviews":["ordinary","skill"]},{"case":"fin-goog","model":"luna","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},{"case":"loblolly-deceleration","model":"sonnet","rep":1,"reviews":["ordinary","skill"]},{"case":"loblolly-matched-lots","model":"terra","rep":1,"reviews":["skill","ordinary"]},